Draw a filled circle on the Bitmap Display using a brute-force approach: for every pixel in the square bounding box around the circle's center, check whether that pixel's distance from the center is within the radius. If it is, color it in.

Why brute-force distance-check instead of a "real" circle algorithm (like Bresenham/midpoint circle)? Those algorithms are more efficient but need more careful integer bookkeeping. The distance-check approach is simpler to reason about and only needs one comparison per pixel: is dx² + dy² ≤ radius²? This avoids sqrt entirely — squaring both sides of the distance formula keeps everything in integers.

Setup:

Pick a center (cx, cy) and a radius.
Loop y from cy - radius to cy + radius.
For each y, loop x from cx - radius to cx + radius.
For each (x, y), compute dx = x - cx, dy = y - cy, and check dx*dx + dy*dy <= radius*radius.
If true, that pixel is inside the circle — compute its framebuffer address using the same formula from the line example, and store the color there.


.data
WIDTH:  .word 512               # display width, must match Bitmap Display tool setting
BASE:   .word 0x10010000          # bitmap base address
COLOR:  .word 0xFF00FF00            # fully opaque green (0xAARRGGBB)
CX:     .word 100                     # circle center x
CY:     .word 100                       # circle center y
RADIUS: .word 30                          # circle radius

.text
.globl main            # .globl makes "main" the program's entry point
main:
    la  t0, WIDTH             # la = Load Address. t0 = address of WIDTH variable
    lw  s1, 0(t0)                # lw = Load Word. s1 = width (loaded ONCE, reused every pixel)

    la  t0, BASE               # la = Load Address. t0 = address of BASE variable
    lw  s0, 0(t0)                 # lw = Load Word. s0 = bitmap base address (loaded ONCE)

    la  t0, COLOR                # la = Load Address. t0 = address of COLOR variable
    lw  s2, 0(t0)                   # lw = Load Word. s2 = color value (loaded ONCE)

    la  t0, CX                     # la = Load Address. t0 = address of CX variable
    lw  s3, 0(t0)                     # lw = Load Word. s3 = cx (circle center x)

    la  t0, CY                       # la = Load Address. t0 = address of CY variable
    lw  s4, 0(t0)                       # lw = Load Word. s4 = cy (circle center y)

    la  t0, RADIUS                     # la = Load Address. t0 = address of RADIUS variable
    lw  s5, 0(t0)                         # lw = Load Word. s5 = radius

    mul s6, s5, s5                    # mul = integer multiply. s6 = radius * radius (r_sq, precomputed once, reused every pixel)

    sub t0, s4, s5                # sub = integer subtract. t0 = y_start = cy - radius (top of bounding box)
    add t1, s4, s5                  # add = integer add. t1 = y_end = cy + radius (bottom of bounding box)

y_loop:
    bgt t0, t1, done                 # bgt = Branch if Greater Than. If y_start(t0) > y_end(t1), all rows done -> exit

    sub t2, s3, s5                      # sub = integer subtract. t2 = x_start = cx - radius (left of bounding box, reset each row)
    add t3, s3, s5                         # add = integer add. t3 = x_end = cx + radius (right of bounding box, reset each row)

x_loop:
    bgt t2, t3, next_y                   # bgt = Branch if Greater Than. If x_start(t2) > x_end(t3), this row done -> move to next row

    sub t4, t2, s3                          # sub = integer subtract. t4 = dx = x - cx
    sub t5, t0, s4                            # sub = integer subtract. t5 = dy = y - cy
    mul t4, t4, t4                              # mul = integer multiply. t4 = dx * dx
    mul t5, t5, t5                                # mul = integer multiply. t5 = dy * dy
    add t6, t4, t5                                  # add = integer add. t6 = dist_sq = dx² + dy²

    bgt t6, s6, skip_pixel                             # bgt = Branch if Greater Than. If dist_sq > r_sq, pixel is OUTSIDE circle -> skip drawing it

    mul a0, t0, s1                    # mul = integer multiply. a0 = y * width
    add a0, a0, t2                       # add = integer add. a0 = y*width + x
    slli a0, a0, 2                          # slli = Shift Left Logical Immediate. a0 = (y*width+x) * 4 (word = 4 bytes)
    add a0, a0, s0                             # add = integer add. a0 = base + byte offset -> final framebuffer address
    sw  s2, 0(a0)                                 # sw = Store Word. Write the color value directly into the framebuffer -> pixel appears instantly (no polling needed)

skip_pixel:
    addi t2, t2, 1                # addi = Add Immediate. x = x + 1 (move to next column)
    j   x_loop                       # j = unconditional Jump. Repeat for next x

next_y:
    addi t0, t0, 1                # addi = Add Immediate. y = y + 1 (move to next row)
    j   y_loop                       # j = unconditional Jump. Repeat for next row

done:
    li  a7, 10                # li = Load Immediate. a7 = 10, syscall code for "exit"
    ecall                          # ecall = Environment Call. Terminates the program
